# ENVIRONMENT — read before running any command

These are verified facts about this machine, not suggestions. Checking them costs nothing;
guessing costs a reinstall loop or a hung command.

## What this box is — and is not

This is the **Linux analysis / simulation** workstation. The firmware toolchain (Keil uVision,
ARMCC V5.06, CMSIS-DAP/SWD probe, `OBJ/`, `*.hex`, `*.axf`) lives on a separate Windows box.
**Never attempt to build, flash, or talk to the target from here** — `tasks.py verify`,
`ground_station.flashtool.rebuild_and_flash`, and the live `livewatch` probe path all require
the Windows box plus a physical probe. The Gazebo lane and `sim/` analysis are this box's job.
The governing principle, restated from the MBD workflow: `sim/` is normative for the algorithm,
firmware is normative for integration.

The Windows box still exists and still produces commits. Notes that mention `schtasks`,
PowerShell 5.1, `.venv/Scripts/python.exe`, `OBJ/JX_FLY.axf`, or `keilkilll.bat` are talking
about **that** machine, not this one. Do not run those commands here.

## Start here: `tasks.py`

Every routine operation has one canonical form. **Use it instead of composing your own
invocation** — the runner always re-launches under the venv interpreter, so a task cannot land
on the wrong Python regardless of shell or activation state.

```bash
.venv/bin/python tasks.py doctor      # FIRST COMMAND OF ANY FRESH SESSION
.venv/bin/python tasks.py test        # full suite
.venv/bin/python tasks.py test sim    # one lane: livewatch | gui | sim | flight
.venv/bin/python tasks.py graph       # rebuild the graphify code graph
.venv/bin/python tasks.py freeze      # regenerate requirements.txt
# NOTE: tasks.py verify lives on the Windows box — do not run here.
```

`doctor` answers, in one command, everything below this line — which interpreter, which
packages, whether git will hang, whether the probe is reachable. Run it before asking the
operator anything about the environment. It exits non-zero only on real problems; an absent
probe is reported as a note, since it blocks `verify` only.

## Python — always use the venv interpreter explicitly

Target is **Python 3.12+ (3.13 preferred)**. `scipy==1.18.0` in `requirements.txt` will not
install on 3.11. There is no `python` symlink on most distros by default; either install
`python-is-python3` or fix the hook commands in `.cursor/hooks.json` / `.claude/settings.json`.
Until then, use `python3` explicitly when creating the venv.

```bash
python3.13 -m venv .venv
.venv/bin/python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
```

There are at least two Pythons on PATH, and one of them is the venv that *should* be activated
but isn't guaranteed to be:

```text
.venv/bin/python             <- has every dependency (auto-activated, but not guaranteed)
/usr/bin/python3             <- system, no deps
```

If you run bare `python` (or `python3`) in a shell where the venv did not activate, you land on
the **system interpreter with no dependencies**, get `ModuleNotFoundError`, and "fix" it by
installing into the wrong interpreter. That is the reinstall loop. Avoid it by always writing
the full path:

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q
.venv/bin/python -m ground_station.livewatch manifests
```

A `ModuleNotFoundError` is far more likely to be *the wrong interpreter* than a missing
package. Check `sys.executable` before installing anything.

`pytest.ini` pins `testpaths`, so a bare `pytest` from the repo root collects every lane
instead of inferring rootdir per-invocation.

## Packages — check before you install

`requirements.txt` is a full pinned freeze of the venv (57 packages). **Read it before running
any install.** Already present, current versions:

| Package  | Version | | Package    | Version |
|----------|---------|-|------------|---------|
| pyocd    | 0.45.1  | | numpy      | 2.4.4   |
| pyserial | 3.5     | | scipy      | 1.18.0  |
| pytest   | 9.0.3   | | matplotlib | 3.10.8  |
| PyYAML   | 6.0.3   | |            |         |

Rules:

- **Never** run `pip install` for something already in `requirements.txt`. Check first.
- If something is genuinely missing, install it into the venv explicitly
  (`.venv/bin/python -m pip install X`), then regenerate the freeze:
  `.venv/bin/python -m pip freeze > requirements.txt`.
- Never `pip install --user`, never install into a system Python, never `sudo`.
- CocoIndex (`ccc search`) and the graphify graph (`.venv/bin/python tasks.py graph`) are
  regenerable and both gitignored — reinstall separately if you want them.
- `DEEPSEEK_API_KEY` is deliberately **not** in the sync repo — export it in your shell
  profile.

## Git — the pager is the thing that hangs you

`core.pager` must be set to `cat` in a fresh clone or `git log` / `git diff` / `git show` will
open a pager and block until the tool times out. Run once per clone:

```bash
git config core.pager cat
```

Belt and braces, since the setting is repo-local and won't follow a fresh clone or worktree:

```bash
git --no-pager log --oneline -20
git --no-pager diff --stat
```

Other git notes for this repo:

- Interactive flags (`-i`) do not work in this environment. No `rebase -i`, no `add -i`.
- The tree carries large build artifacts; prefer `--stat` or explicit paths over bare
  `git diff` on the whole tree.
- `git stash` is **repo-level and shared across worktrees**. Do not use it as a checkpoint
  when more than one agent may be running. Use a scratch-branch commit instead.
- **An hourly scheduled task (`UAV-autopush`) on the Windows box pushes that box's branch to
  origin.** So commits can reach the remote without anyone running `git push` — that is
  expected, not a sign someone else pushed. It only ever pushes existing commits: it never
  commits, stages, force-pushes, or touches the working tree. Log: `.agent_state/autopush.log`.
  Remove on the Windows box with `schtasks /Delete /TN "UAV-autopush" /F`.
- Because commits reach the remote within the hour, **do not amend or rebase a commit that is
  more than an hour old** without checking `git log @{u}..` first.

## Shell — bash, with one space-trap

Bash is the only shell here. No PowerShell, no CMD. Stick to POSIX syntax.

- `&&`, `||`, `[[ ]]`, `$(...)`, process substitution all work normally.
- `head`, `tail`, `which`, `touch`, `realpath` exist (unlike on Windows PowerShell).
- One trap: paths are clean on this box, no spaces. (The Windows path
  `FreeRTOS---Six_Degrees_of_Freedom _Adaptive_controller` has one — that is a Windows-only
  fact and not relevant here.) Still: quote paths in case anything gets moved or symlinked.

## When a command fails

Diagnose before escalating. The failures that account for most of it on this box:

1. Wrong Python interpreter (see above).
2. `core.pager` not yet set on a fresh clone — `git log` hangs.
3. Trying to use a Windows-only tool (`schtasks`, `keilkilll.bat`, `OBJ/JX_FLY.axf`, `pyocd`
   against a probe that is not here).

None of these are reasons to stop the task or ask the operator for help — fix and retry.
See `.cursor/rules/hardware-safety.mdc` for the (short) list of things that genuinely require
stopping.